{
 "experiment_id": "E0076",
 "line_id": "L0018",
 "kind": "pilot (not a judgement): can E0076 loops be judged on onset at all? One f* (0.38), both widths.",
 "made_at": "2026-11-25",
 "sources": {
  "width100": "A0068:main-3d-f0.38/step-*/fields.npz (E0073 baseline loop, L 100)",
  "width200": "A0185:out/step-*/fields.npz (E0076 job 164691, L 200)",
  "note": "fetched to tmp and deleted after the run; sha256 of each file in the rows"
 },
 "measure": "Ad hoc, for this pilot only; not a decided onset rule. From each step's fields.npz: the height variance at each of the 32 phases of the cycle (mean over the step's frames at that phase); amp_D = square root of the largest of these, in sphere diameters; at that phase, the ring of the height spectrum (k in units of 2 pi / L) that carries most variance (ring_k_at_max) and its share of the total (ring_share_at_max). var_min_phase is the smallest phase-mean variance.",
 "reading": [
  "Pattern wavelength is the same at both widths: the dominant ring is k = 4 at width 100 (wavelength 25 sphere diameters) and k = 8 to 9 at width 200 (22 to 25 diameters), from Gamma 2.5 to 3.9.",
  "On the way down the pattern-wavelength ring stops being dominant between Gamma 2.5 and 2.45 at both widths: at 2.45 the dominant ring is long-wave (k = 2 at width 100, k = 5 at width 200) with about 0.2 of the variance, against 0.3 to 0.65 for the pattern ring above.",
  "On the way up the pattern ring is dominant again from Gamma 2.65 at width 100 (share 0.38, 0.55 at 2.7) and from 2.7 at width 200 (share 0.29, k = 8; k = 9 from 2.85). So under this measure the return is 0.05 apart and the down/up gap is about 0.15 to 0.2 in Gamma at both widths.",
  "amp_D falls smoothly to the floor at both widths and has no flat level: it includes the whole-layer expansion, so it is not by itself a pattern amplitude. A rule for E0076 should use the pattern ring, not amp_D alone.",
  "One f*, one seed, an undecided measure: no claim. It shows that these loops do lose and regain the pattern within their working range at f* 0.38, so the registered onset test can be applied where the floor is below about 2.5."
 ],
